 {
  "version": "1.3.6",
  "date": "2026-10-30",
  "sections": [
   {
    "title": "What's fixed",
    "lines": [
     {
      "kind": "bullet",
      "text": "**Karousel no longer lets the music stop at the end of the queue.** Karousel used to look for more music only once, when the last song started. If your server sent nothing back, it tried again only after that song had ended. iOS suspends an app that goes quiet in the background, so the music often stopped before Karousel found anything. Now Karousel keeps looking every 15 seconds while the last song plays. Its songs are lined up and mixed in like any others, so the music doesn't pause at the end of the queue."
     },
     {
      "kind": "bullet",
      "text": "**When the queue runs out, Kultr waits for the search that is already running** instead of starting a new one. At that point it has usually already found something."
     },
     {
      "kind": "bullet",
      "text": "**Karousel uses your downloads when the server can't be reached.** Before each search Kultr now checks whether your server is answering, not just whether the phone is online. If the server is at home and you're out, Karousel picks only downloaded songs from your library: same artists, same genres, what you play most and have hearted. Everything it adds plays without the server. Before, it waited for a server that couldn't answer, then picked songs that couldn't stream."
     },
     {
      "kind": "bullet",
      "text": "**A slow server can't hold Karousel up.** Karousel waits up to 20 seconds in total for your server's similar songs and artists, then fills in from your library. Before, each request could take up to a minute."
     },
     {
      "kind": "bullet",
      "text": "Karousel songs found for a queue you've since replaced are no longer added to the new one."
     },
     {
      "kind": "text",
      "text": "The playback log (Settings → About) now also shows each Karousel search and how long it took."
     }
    ]
   }
  ]
 },
 {
  "version": "1.3.5",
  "date": "2026-10-27",
  "sections": [
   {
    "title": "What's fixed",
    "lines": [
     {
      "kind": "bullet",
      "text": "**Music that goes quiet in the background comes back by itself.** iOS keeps an app running in the background only while it makes sound, and something could still silence Kultr mid-song: iOS stopping the player, or a stream that stalled without failing. Kultr waited, and after half a minute of quiet iOS suspended it, so the music stopped until you pressed play. Now, when Kultr should be playing but has been silent for four seconds, it tells the player to play again. If that doesn't work by twelve seconds, it reloads the song where it stopped. Both happen well before iOS would suspend it."
     },
     {
      "kind": "bullet",
      "text": "**A stalled stream gives up sooner.** A connection that stops sending without dropping used to hang for a minute, longer than iOS lets a quiet app run. It now fails after 20 seconds, so Kultr can retry the song while it is still running."
     },
     {
      "kind": "bullet",
      "text": "**Lighter in the background.** Kultr frees its cover-art memory when you leave it. Of the apps playing in the background, iOS closes the largest first when it needs memory."
     },
     {
      "kind": "bullet",
      "text": "**The library check iOS runs in the background now finishes on time.** If it ran out of time, iOS could close Kultr while music was playing."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "title": "New: Playback log",
    "lines": [
     {
      "kind": "text",
      "text": "**Settings → About → Playback log** records what played, every pause and its cause (Lock Screen, headphones, a call, iOS), and what iOS did to Kultr in the background. That includes memory warnings, suspensions, crashes and the cases where iOS closed it. If the music still stops by itself, open the log, tap the share button and send it to me, so I can see exactly what happened."
     }
    ]
   }
  ]
 },
